From 06ce11e3346d6c50ae6d867c031c6eea3b6dda53 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ymote <151983+ymote@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 17:03:35 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(transport): never drop a server reply
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A reply, an error reply or a connection-state change was handed to the
consumer with `try_send` (`try_emit`) and dropped with a warning whenever
the bounded event channel (`CHANNEL_BUFFER` = 64) was full; notifications
already waited for room (`send().await`). A consumer that counts its
requests never heard the dropped reply and stayed one reply behind for
good. Seen in a client of this crate (OctosCode): another client streams
into a Session while it is being opened, the history reply lands on a full
channel and is dropped, the Session never leaves "Loading conversation…",
and its next history reply is judged against the lost read.

Every event now goes through `emit`, which waits for room in the channel.
When the consumer is slow, the read loop waits and TCP flow control
reaches the server, as it already did for notifications. Delivery ends
only when the consumer is gone (the receiver dropped), and then there is
nobody left to tell. `handle_response` and `fail_all_pending` become
async; the ws and kernel loops await their state changes.

Tests (proto.rs): a reply that finds the channel full waits and is
delivered; replies of every kind (history reads, an error reply,
session/list, turn/start) arrive in the server's order through a one-slot
channel and a slow consumer, interleaved with notifications. Both fail
before this change: every reply is dropped.
